{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>These are the generations of Noah.</b> The verse's saying <b>these</b> indicates the exclusion of all others. One might say that it intends to disqualify his generation, except that there was no need to inform of this since they had already been disqualified and sealed for destruction. Rather, it may be explained according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 30:6): the principal generations of the righteous are commandments and good deeds — end quote. They were precise in saying ״the principal״ and did not suffice for them to say that even good deeds are attributed to them with the name ״generations,״ to thereby construe the word ״these״ — meaning, these are the principal thing for the righteous. And he disqualified the sons mentioned in the preceding section, as it is written ״וַיּוֹלֶד נֹחַ״ etc." "It may further be explained according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Deuteronomy Rabbah 11:3), and this is their language: Noah says to Moses, I am greater than you; and Moses says to him, you saved yourself but did not have the power to save your generation — end quote. And this is the verse's saying <b>these are the generations of Noah</b> — Noah alone, and the rest is spoils, for his merits were of no benefit to the children of his generation and helped only himself. And with this the repetition of ״Noah״ is settled. Also the verse's saying <b>in his generations</b> is to say that his merits helped nothing at all to better his generations and turn them toward the good, but only himself and his sons, who are a branch of him and are included in his name. It further intends to magnify Noah's praise, for he is unique in this praise beyond those who came before him; for although righteous men preceded him, they would learn discipline and understanding from the sons of the righteous generations, whereas Noah — though all his generations were wicked — was righteous. And corresponding to this detail the verse says ״these״ to exclude those who came before him, for they are not on his level." "<b>Further,</b> the entire verse may be explained along this path: <b>these are the generations of Noah</b> etc. — meaning, these are the order of his generations, what he produced through his good deeds by his agency. First: Noah, and this is according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory in Genesis Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 25:2), and this is their language: at the time the Holy One blessed be He created man, He granted him dominion over all — a cow would obey the plowman's furrow etc.; they rebelled against him etc.; once Noah came etc.; Resh Lakish said, before Noah the waters would rise and flood them from their graves; once Noah came they rested etc. — end quote. This is what is enumerated in the order of his generations by saying ״Noah״ twice. A second generation — <b>a man</b>, meaning his being a man of the soil, and this is according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Tanhuma, Genesis 11): ״וּמֵעִצְּבוֹן יָדֵינוּ״ — before Noah came they had no plow implements and he prepared them for them etc. — end quote. And this is what he alluded to in the word <b>a man.</b> Third: <b>righteous</b>, alluding that he is the foundation of the world, according to the verse (Proverbs 10:25): ״וְצַדִּיק יְסוֹד עוֹלָם״ — for were it not for him the world would not exist and everything would have been as nothing in the flood, and in him HaShem founded His world. Fourth: <b>wholehearted</b>, according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory in Ḥullin folio 6 (Zevaḥim 116, Avodah Zarah 10), and this is their language: righteous in his deeds, wholehearted in his ways; and Rashi explained, and this is his language: in his ways — humble and lowly of spirit — end quote. This declares the intensity of his confirmation that even in his wicked generations, in all of them he was humble etc., and this is beyond the line of justice, for the line of justice dictates ״עִם חָסִיד״ etc. ״וְעִם עִקֵּשׁ״ etc. (with the devout… and with the crooked), and he did not act so. And furthermore, even in the eyes of his generations his humility was recognized, and this is the verse's saying <b>wholehearted in his generations.</b> And it speaks in the plural to declare his excellence. Now, every person attains recognition of three generations: the first, the generation of his father; the second, his own generation; and the third, the generation of his sons. And Scripture declares that in all of them he was distinguished. And although Methuselah was present, he alone remained of the earlier ones who were before him. Fifth: <b>et HaElohim</b> etc., meaning that even with HaShem he conducted gentleness and restfulness, and this is according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 25), and this is their language: Rabbi Eliezer says, he is called by the name of his offering, as it is stated ״וַיָּרַח ה׳ אֶת רֵיחַ הַנִּיחֹחַ״ — end quote. And therefore he returned to say ״Noah״ a second time, and did not suffice with what he had mentioned at the beginning of the verse. And he spoke in the language of walking, for so is said also regarding good conduct, in the manner of the verse (Deuteronomy 28:9) ״וְהָלַכְתָּ בִּדְרָכָיו״. And he was precise in saying <b>walked</b>, to say that with constancy he would please his Maker blessed be He, and his offering testifies to the general principle. And with this all the fine points are settled. Also the verse's saying ״these״ is because among the preceding ones there is none comparable in his generations."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {2 "The abbreviation וכו׳ (etc.) is rendered as such throughout, preserving the Or HaChaim's practice of truncating midrashic quotations. The parenthetical Talmudic references (Zevaḥim 116, Avodah Zarah 10) appear to be the Or HaChaim's own cross-references to the passage he cites from Ḥullin folio 6."}, 